<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Nevsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1221">1221</a>–<a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Nevsky was a Rus' prince who served as prince of Novgorod and from 1252 as Grand Prince of Vladimir. He defeated the Swedes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> in 1240 and the knights of the Livonian Order on the ice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a> in 1242. He chose submission to the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> to protect his lands, and was later made a saint of the Russian Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky.jpg" alt="Alexander Nevsky" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collective of Kremlin Armory artists · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlexanderNevskyTitul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the death of <b>Alexander Nevsky</b> in 1263 the town passed, according to tradition, to his youngest son Daniel, then a small child, and under him it became the seat of a separate line of princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Alexander Yaroslavich</b> defeated a Swedish force on the Neva in 1240, earning the name Nevsky, and in 1242 defeated the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order on the ice of Lake Peipus, the Battle on the Ice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After that the grand princes fought chiefly as agents or rivals under Mongol overlordship, while <b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, before his accession, had defeated the Swedes on the Neva in 1240 and the Teutonic Knights on Lake Peipus in 1242.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod's forces under Prince <b>Alexander Nevsky</b> beat the Livonian branch of the Teutonic Order in the Battle on the Ice, halting the Order's eastward expansion into Russian lands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Novgorodian army led by <b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, with Karelian allies, defeated a Swedish force that included Norwegian, Finnish and Tavastian troops on the Neva River near Ust-Izhora.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-15">15 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Tver, on the upper Volga, received its first prince around 1247, when Yaroslav Yaroslavich, a younger brother of <b>Alexander Nevsky</b>, made it his seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-tver">Grand Principality of Tver</a></li></ul></section>
